# Compiled the malware C++ file in Linux
x86_64-w64-mingw32-g++ caldera_exploit.cpp -o steam.exe \
    -lws2_32 -lwininet -liphlpapi -lwinhttp \
    -static-libgcc -static-libstdc++ -static

# This approach of malware I was learnt through my research on filess malware. At this point, the payload is dynamic, 
# attacker can change the payload when ever they want and they still can use the old malware. And the obfuscation layers, the analysis just have the
# C++ binary file, and this file just contain the download logic, not the  payload, the analysis can analize the decode things, but attacker 
# can change the server whenever they want, so analysis will not be able to get the payload. This approach is best for metasploit or any tools like that,
# in this project I used Caldera, so I have to do for it. Because Caldera will leave a file in the victim machine when the command is executed,
# so that the defender will discover it instantly, if we use the C2 server like metasploit, it just leave a connection to the server and we can 
# execute command from the shell that we have gotten.